1
00:00:01,120 --> 00:00:08,720
Welcome to the Subclub Podcast, a show dedicated to the best practices for building and growing app businesses.

2
00:00:09,920 --> 00:00:19,280
We sit down with the entrepreneurs, investors and builders behind the most successful apps in the world to learn from their successes and failures.

3
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:22,480
Subclub is brought to you by RevenueCat.

4
00:00:23,280 --> 00:00:32,720
Thousands of the world's best apps trust RevenueCat to power in app purchases, manage customers, and grow revenue across iOS, Android, and the web.

5
00:00:33,680 --> 00:00:38,240
You can learn more at revenuecat dot com dot Let's get into the show.

6
00:00:41,600 --> 00:00:43,520
Hello, I'm your host, David Bernard.

7
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:48,480
My guest today is Jim Kantsu, Chief Product Officer at Duolingo.

8
00:00:49,120 --> 00:01:02,960
On the podcast, I talk with Jim about the premium trap many apps fall into, why free trials work even for freeman products, and how Try for zero dollars and zero cents actually outperforms Try for free.

9
00:01:03,680 --> 00:01:06,080
Hey, Jim, thanks so much for joining me on the podcast today.

10
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:07,040
Happy to be here.

11
00:01:07,280 --> 00:01:07,920
Excited to chat.

12
00:01:08,320 --> 00:01:11,360
So I wanted to talk through Duolingo's monetization story.

13
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:18,480
I mean, few apps I out there will have tens of millions of users and be in the position Duolingo was in when you started monetizing.

14
00:01:18,640 --> 00:01:21,920
But I think there's so much to learn from the lessons you all learned along the way.

15
00:01:22,560 --> 00:01:23,440
So let's talk about that.

16
00:01:23,680 --> 00:01:26,960
Duolingo was a famously free app, completely free.

17
00:01:27,680 --> 00:01:30,240
And then the first step was layering on ads.

18
00:01:30,480 --> 00:01:32,560
It was actually a website, it was not a mobile app.

19
00:01:33,040 --> 00:01:36,720
And this website's idea was actually to be completely free.

20
00:01:36,880 --> 00:01:41,280
So there would actually be no monetization for the users that are using Duolingo to learn a language.

21
00:01:41,760 --> 00:01:47,600
However, as users did exercises to learn a language, which includes a lot of translation.

22
00:01:47,680 --> 00:01:54,560
So if you're learning Spanish, a lot of your exercises, you're translating Spanish to English or English to Spanish.

23
00:01:54,960 --> 00:02:02,560
Users would do these exercises to learn, but there would be a crowdsourced model to collect those translations and eventually turn that into a translation service.

24
00:02:03,600 --> 00:02:05,200
We actually did this, like this was built.

25
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:08,480
So I heard the story, but I didn't know you actually did this.

26
00:02:09,680 --> 00:02:10,080
This went live.

27
00:02:10,640 --> 00:02:13,040
And it actually generated some revenue, even though very small.

28
00:02:13,920 --> 00:02:23,600
So we would have customers that are like at the time, like the big news outlets as well, like CNN, Buzzfeed, they would feed an article they wanted to translate from English to Spanish.

29
00:02:24,240 --> 00:02:35,840
And then our users in bits and pieces would translate that article and we would crowdsource a translated article and then provide a translated article back to CNN.

30
00:02:37,040 --> 00:02:38,160
It was actually a genius model.

31
00:02:38,640 --> 00:02:44,400
If you want to do something crowdsourced, it's actually you're kind of serving both sides a positive value.

32
00:02:45,200 --> 00:02:48,080
The challenge with that model was, though, is twofold.

33
00:02:48,320 --> 00:02:49,360
One of them is translation.

34
00:02:49,920 --> 00:02:51,520
Also, this is pre LLMs.

35
00:02:51,920 --> 00:02:56,560
Translation now has changed a lot post LLMs, but this is, you have to remember this is 2013 as well.

36
00:02:57,360 --> 00:03:00,320
Even then, translation was a race to the bottom business model.

37
00:03:00,880 --> 00:03:12,720
So if we were providing a service for, I don't know, how three cents, ten cents, three dollars, whatever it is, there was always someone else trying to do it for a little cheaper by having someone in the Philippines translate it for cheaper.

38
00:03:12,880 --> 00:03:14,240
So there's always a race to the bottom.

39
00:03:14,480 --> 00:03:16,000
It is hard to scale for that reason.

40
00:03:16,480 --> 00:03:22,800
And then number two is more fundamental, which is the translation quality of a language learner is actually not great.

41
00:03:23,600 --> 00:03:35,040
So you end up putting practices in place to level the quality of that translation, which then you turn into a basically a translation business and then really start shifting your resources towards basically translation and not teaching.

42
00:03:35,840 --> 00:03:47,680
So we realized that it had these problems and around the time I joined Duolingo, which was 2016, we had pivoted away from that model and was trying to figure out what the next model should be.

43
00:03:48,080 --> 00:04:04,320
Actually, our very, very first experiment with anything monetization was the streak repair as an inap purchase and around the same time the ads experiment started, but I think the first thing that users saw at Duolingo as like a thing they could buy was repairing your streak for I think a dollar or three dollars.

44
00:04:04,640 --> 00:04:05,680
That was our first entry.

45
00:04:06,320 --> 00:04:07,920
It was honestly, it was a good experiment.

46
00:04:08,400 --> 00:04:09,280
We actually learned a lot.

47
00:04:09,360 --> 00:04:11,520
We learned that users really cared about their streak.

48
00:04:12,640 --> 00:04:17,680
A good percentage of our users are willing to pay a dollar or two to repair their broken streak.

49
00:04:18,400 --> 00:04:29,360
But then I think then we got into this experiment mode of what really works with our audience and how do we make sure that we keep the product highly retentive and a great user experience.

50
00:04:30,480 --> 00:04:33,360
Coming back to your question, that is the moment when we started trying ads.

51
00:04:33,440 --> 00:04:34,560
So that was 2016.

52
00:04:35,760 --> 00:04:53,440
It's funny if you launched, if you go watch, and I think these talks are still out there, our cofounder CEO, Luis gave these talks for Duolingo's launch and he said, We will never have ads, never in-app purchases and never subscriptions, and those are the exact three things that I built in that order, basically.

53
00:04:54,080 --> 00:05:03,040
And the reason he was so adamant in saying those was we, Duolingo's mission is basically centered around providing access to education.

54
00:05:03,760 --> 00:05:10,240
And at the time, every education business model, whatever they did, they would lock the education value behind a paywall, get someone to pay.

55
00:05:10,640 --> 00:05:15,920
Think about the Rosetta Stone model, for example, that was the very stern model at the time where you're selling content.

56
00:05:16,160 --> 00:05:17,200
We never wanted it to be that.

57
00:05:17,440 --> 00:05:24,400
But I think we realized there's actually a way to build, do that, which is provide access to education, but also monetize the bells and whistles.

58
00:05:24,880 --> 00:05:26,640
That's why we set on this freemium journey.

59
00:05:26,800 --> 00:05:28,320
Ads fits into that really nicely.

60
00:05:28,640 --> 00:05:33,040
If done well, users don't have to pay you anything, but you can actually generate some revenue.

61
00:05:33,360 --> 00:05:35,120
So Ads was that test.

62
00:05:35,600 --> 00:05:37,760
Honestly, testing Ads wasn't rocket science.

63
00:05:37,920 --> 00:05:47,680
It was fairly easy to test our way into, of course, making sure the user experience is great, but also how do you maximize revenue from ads while making sure your attention remains awesome.

64
00:05:48,240 --> 00:06:00,240
The hardest part I would say was the cultural shift of going from a pre-monetization app to a post-monetization app, because until then, so until the time I joined, there was like sixty ish employees.

65
00:06:01,120 --> 00:06:13,920
I think a lot of folks joined the company thinking it was maybe even like a nonprofit like mission, because the mission is so grand and so awesome, and the company never quite bothered to think about revenue and monetization very much.

66
00:06:14,240 --> 00:06:28,640
It was a mindset to the company to be, hey, there's things we have to do so we can generate revenue, and if we generate revenue, we can actually fund our mission so that we can be more impactful was probably the hardest part of turning on ads on Duolingo.

67
00:06:29,280 --> 00:06:35,840
And I guess you weren't there at this time, but I did hear there's a story out there around a venture capitalist pushing this direction.

68
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:36,800
What was the story about?

69
00:06:37,600 --> 00:06:41,680
This story, I think, is when Capital G invested in Duolingo.

70
00:06:42,240 --> 00:06:53,680
Layla, who was also on our board after that investment for a while, she told Luis at the time, this is probably the last investor you will have in this company before you figure out monetization.

71
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:57,200
So in a rounded way to say, you have to figure out monetization.

72
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:00,240
As a growing company, you at some point have to do that.

73
00:07:00,480 --> 00:07:06,960
But I think also the common wisdom at the time for consumer companies like Facebook is like, just grow audience, you'll figure out monetization later.

74
00:07:07,440 --> 00:07:12,640
So I think Duolingo followed that, but until a point where I think Capital G and Layla had this big push to be.

75
00:07:12,800 --> 00:07:14,800
be, okay, it's time.

76
00:07:15,840 --> 00:07:18,240
And I guess that was around 2016.

77
00:07:18,480 --> 00:07:27,920
So when I joined, the mindset shift had moved to, and Luis's first thing he told me was like, we hired you, your sole job is to figure out how to monetize Duolingo.

78
00:07:29,440 --> 00:07:31,360
And that's what I tried to figure out.

79
00:07:31,680 --> 00:07:32,320
That's awesome.

80
00:07:32,560 --> 00:07:33,280
What a great story.

81
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:36,880
So ads, they're tricky.

82
00:07:37,360 --> 00:07:44,480
How did you introduce ads, one, in a way that didn't totally piss off users and tank retention?

83
00:07:44,720 --> 00:07:48,320
And I mean, I'm sure there was user pushback and bad reviews in the App Store.

84
00:07:48,960 --> 00:07:49,760
This is part of the story.

85
00:07:49,920 --> 00:08:00,160
I think a lot of people can learn from because any monetization change you make will spur some level of like bad reviews or negative feedback or churn.

86
00:08:00,880 --> 00:08:04,960
So how did you balance that as you started to roll ads into the product?

87
00:08:05,440 --> 00:08:06,240
This is a very good question.

88
00:08:06,480 --> 00:08:11,040
I think that there's the I'll give the obvious answers and maybe the more difficult to navigate parts.

89
00:08:11,440 --> 00:08:19,920
First, for a consumer product, you should obviously dog food your product heavily so that whatever you're doing to the experience, you have firsthand experience on how it feels.

90
00:08:20,320 --> 00:08:22,960
Meaning, you can easily say, Oh, we'll put an app open ad.

91
00:08:23,200 --> 00:08:24,960
And if you're just watching metrics, I'm not sure.

92
00:08:24,960 --> 00:08:28,480
You're just watching metrics, I'm sure that might look fine, but when you test it, you're like, what the hell is this?

93
00:08:29,120 --> 00:08:34,000
Then you should know what you've introduced and obviously avoid the things that you wouldn't want to see as a user.

94
00:08:34,640 --> 00:08:47,600
Hence, our first test, and honestly, kind of even today, that's our only ad placement is after you've computered a lesson, we show you an ad, not during, not before, but always after, because we believe that doesn't interrupt your learning.

95
00:08:47,920 --> 00:08:53,200
So one is the qualitative, it should be a good user experience, and you should know that by testing the app yourself every day.

96
00:08:53,760 --> 00:09:00,480
The second is quantitative, like measure everything you can, like is retention fine, is app closes fine, what's happening?

97
00:09:01,040 --> 00:09:01,840
to your reviews?

98
00:09:02,480 --> 00:09:03,840
You might have to make some trade-offs.

99
00:09:04,080 --> 00:09:10,080
Ideally, you wouldn't, but realistically, if you go from zero ads to some amount of ads, there is some risk that some curves might come down.

100
00:09:10,560 --> 00:09:12,160
But I think you should know what that cost is.

101
00:09:12,320 --> 00:09:14,160
Anyway, that's like through AB testing.

102
00:09:14,320 --> 00:09:15,680
That's how we measured it to be.

103
00:09:15,920 --> 00:09:22,480
Honestly, our retention was maybe a slight hit, but mostly neutral for most of our ad tests over time.

104
00:09:23,680 --> 00:09:24,160
That's one.

105
00:09:25,040 --> 00:09:30,080
The third one is there is, of course, you go from a free product to selling something.

106
00:09:31,040 --> 00:09:34,000
Users won't always say, thank you for doing that.

107
00:09:34,720 --> 00:09:40,800
Like this, imagine if Uber started completely free and one day they said, Okay, now we're going to charge for our Ubers.

108
00:09:41,360 --> 00:09:42,400
Users would hate that too.

109
00:09:42,720 --> 00:09:44,320
And that was kind of what we did.

110
00:09:44,480 --> 00:09:50,400
I mean, in a way, we provided a free service and we kept a lot of the elements free, but we said, Hey, actually, you can buy a street repair now.

111
00:09:50,720 --> 00:09:53,200
And you might have to see an ad at the end of your session.

112
00:09:54,080 --> 00:09:54,960
We got some complaints.

113
00:09:55,360 --> 00:09:59,520
The thing that helped us have clarity was, one, retention was fine.

114
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:02,400
Retention was neutral on most of these experiments.

115
00:10:03,120 --> 00:10:12,560
And when you looked at our community's complaint volume, if you quantify that, for example, for a million DAUs, how many?

116
00:10:12,720 --> 00:10:14,240
How many ad complaints are there?

117
00:10:14,560 --> 00:10:18,800
Or ad negative reviews, however you want to count that versus other topics.

118
00:10:19,280 --> 00:10:28,160
For example, I realized this quantitative analysis in 2016 because we were at this pivotal moment of, do we launch ads or do we not launch ads?

119
00:10:28,800 --> 00:10:38,400
And we looked at, for example, the number of complaints about why we didn't have the Finnish language on Duolingo was three x the number of people that are complaining about Duolingo having ads.

120
00:10:38,640 --> 00:10:47,840
So that starts putting it into perspective, like it's not zero complaints, but actually in the grand scheme of things, this is not going to break the app and our community is not going to run away tomorrow.

121
00:10:48,160 --> 00:10:56,240
So I think understanding the volume of what you're doing to the community as much as you can gives you perspective.

122
00:10:56,640 --> 00:11:00,240
Otherwise, every negative comment as a product builder hits home really hard.

123
00:11:00,640 --> 00:11:03,120
Like everything looks like the world is falling apart.

124
00:11:03,440 --> 00:11:09,280
That's why it helps to like look at the broad picture of like all of our issues as a product and all products have many issues.

125
00:11:09,520 --> 00:11:09,600
Yeah.

126
00:11:10,240 --> 00:11:13,600
That's exactly what I was going to say, that it's so hard as a builder.

127
00:11:14,080 --> 00:11:14,480
It's really hard.

128
00:11:14,960 --> 00:11:16,800
To get those few negative reviews.

129
00:11:17,040 --> 00:11:22,720
And I think that's a really helpful insight to look at that bigger picture and understand.

130
00:11:22,960 --> 00:11:29,440
Like, I got a bunch of negative reviews for my weather app for the audacity of charging $40 a year for a weather app.

131
00:11:30,160 --> 00:11:33,280
And I did get probably over focused on those negative reviews.

132
00:11:33,600 --> 00:11:38,480
But then when you look at all the people who were subscribing, were staying subscribed, actually had pretty good retention.

133
00:11:38,640 --> 00:11:47,760
It's like you have to like zoom out and not let those like that small subset of users who are going to complain tank what's ultimately going to help drive the business forward.

134
00:11:48,320 --> 00:11:50,720
How did you work on ad quality?

135
00:11:51,680 --> 00:11:53,520
I believe you implemented AdMob.

136
00:11:54,800 --> 00:11:57,280
Again, in my weather app, I had AdMob.

137
00:11:57,520 --> 00:12:05,120
Eventually, I ended up pulling it because it was like gambling ads with, you know, 777 and animations and like all this crap.

138
00:12:05,520 --> 00:12:13,840
How did you work with AdMob and how do you think about ad quality as part of the experience and how it can actually be detrimental to the experience?

139
00:12:14,160 --> 00:12:23,200
Yeah, I mean, ad quality, we I would say we're like incredibly strict on, but with programmatic, you'll of course have some amount of control, not full control.

140
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:28,400
But with anything that is, honestly, it could be even slightly dicey, we blocked all those categories.

141
00:12:28,640 --> 00:12:33,040
Like if it's gambling, even if it's like games that look kind of scary, pick your category.

142
00:12:33,360 --> 00:12:36,880
Any of those that we didn't feel was also young audience safe.

143
00:12:37,520 --> 00:12:39,520
and or just brand safe, we blocked.

144
00:12:39,680 --> 00:12:49,520
So the starting point is we used AdMob first and then we integrated other sources like Facebook Ads or at the time Flurry and now we use also I think multiple networks.

145
00:12:49,600 --> 00:12:50,240
We use Unity.

146
00:12:51,120 --> 00:13:00,640
That was to mainly broaden demand, but for category and what kind of ads we allow, we tried our best to block off any categories that are dysfunny, even though it might be a revenue loss.

147
00:13:00,880 --> 00:13:01,840
We're like, we don't care.

148
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:04,960
The most important thing is good user experience and ad quality.

149
00:13:05,200 --> 00:13:12,400
So we did a lot of category blocking and if we needed to custom blocking, we went and tried to work with ad networks to be like this type of ad.

150
00:13:12,960 --> 00:13:14,560
has like three X buttons to exit.

151
00:13:14,800 --> 00:13:15,760
We don't want this in our app.

152
00:13:16,080 --> 00:13:17,200
Please don't give us this thing.

153
00:13:17,600 --> 00:13:20,000
So we also did some of that back and forth as well.

154
00:13:20,640 --> 00:13:21,280
That's our stance.

155
00:13:21,680 --> 00:13:28,640
This year also to improve ad quality, we're also doing our own direct ads more and more because then we control quality entirely.

156
00:13:29,200 --> 00:13:33,760
It's an advertiser that we know, like we shook hands with, and the format is what we've created.

157
00:13:34,160 --> 00:13:36,400
So it's beautiful and it's a great advertiser.

158
00:13:36,720 --> 00:13:43,760
So one now new lever for us is to reduce programmatic volume and increase direct volume because it's just a better user experience.

159
00:13:43,840 --> 00:13:43,920
Yeah.

160
00:13:44,240 --> 00:13:45,200
I love what you're doing.

161
00:13:45,360 --> 00:13:47,600
I mean, it's hard for smaller apps.

162
00:13:47,680 --> 00:13:56,000
You don't have scale to to do this kind of thing, but tell me more about how these partnerships work to improve that ad quality so that it does improve the user experience.

163
00:13:56,080 --> 00:14:00,640
Because you've been incorporating Duolingo characters into the ads with the brands you're partnering with.

164
00:14:01,040 --> 00:14:01,600
Have you seen them?

165
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:03,760
I haven't seen them yet, but I read about them.

166
00:14:03,920 --> 00:14:04,320
It's very cool.

167
00:14:04,960 --> 00:14:14,240
It's currently only in the US, so we're focusing on growing these in the US because obviously we create a great format, but to be able to scale this, we also need to be working with companies.

168
00:14:14,400 --> 00:14:17,840
And right now we're just focused on companies that would want to advertise with us in the US.

169
00:14:18,320 --> 00:14:24,000
We created multiple formats, but the one I love the most and that seems to be working really well is one of our characters.

170
00:14:24,400 --> 00:14:29,920
So we have eight Duolingo characters on Duolingo that you see is introduces the ad.

171
00:14:30,320 --> 00:14:31,920
For example, pick up brand.

172
00:14:32,240 --> 00:14:34,160
Let's say it's a detergent.

173
00:14:34,320 --> 00:14:35,040
I'm making this up.

174
00:14:35,120 --> 00:14:36,560
It's not like we had exactly this.

175
00:14:36,880 --> 00:14:41,840
But one of our characters, Lily, would make jokes about like, Oh, like, I need to wash my clothes.

176
00:14:42,080 --> 00:14:42,960
I wish I could do that.

177
00:14:43,360 --> 00:14:46,800
And we kind of write a custom script to introduce that ad that's very short.

178
00:14:47,200 --> 00:14:48,480
And then the ad itself plays.

179
00:14:49,040 --> 00:14:52,880
So that at least we are building a little bit of our character world further.

180
00:14:53,520 --> 00:14:56,000
And it's not jarring as like you entered a lesson right now.

181
00:14:56,240 --> 00:14:58,880
That's what our ads are really a little bit where like you see an ad.

182
00:14:59,200 --> 00:15:00,720
It feels like you're easing into the ad.

183
00:15:01,280 --> 00:15:02,240
We're trying this right now.

184
00:15:02,480 --> 00:15:07,120
It's when you see it as a user, it's actually like a, as an ad, it's a very delightful format.

185
00:15:07,600 --> 00:15:09,200
Our challenge, I would say, is scaling it.

186
00:15:09,360 --> 00:15:14,640
I think we have, since it's a custom format, well, you have to go up to advertise and explain how this works.

187
00:15:14,720 --> 00:15:25,200
So it's a bit of a slow adoption process, but we're going to, I mean, our goal is to basically increase the volume of these ads and reduce the programmatic by a good amount in 2026.

188
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:30,720
One of the ads you do often show is for the subscription itself.

189
00:15:31,200 --> 00:15:35,280
How do you balance from an LTV perspective and monetization perspective?

190
00:15:35,360 --> 00:15:42,720
How do you balance when to show those ads for the subscription and when to show programmatic or these new brand ads?

191
00:15:43,360 --> 00:16:12,640
So we just to set the stage, if you do a Duolingo lesson, and this includes any of our lessons, so you could do language, math, music, and chess, at the end of that lesson you see an ad, and we decide, like you said, if we should show you a programmatic ad versus one of our internal ads, which could be an ad for a subscription, or it could also be an ad for our other products, like the Duolingo English test, but we call those basically internal ads that we decide we've produced, and it is a Duolingo product.

192
00:16:13,520 --> 00:16:18,240
Most of them are going to be either Super Duolingo ads or Duolingo Max ads.

193
00:16:19,040 --> 00:16:37,040
The decision logic is mostly now controlled by ML actually, but the ML uses various signals that is trying to figure out LTV maximization at its core, and it is looking at every signal that we feed it that could be an indicator for someone that is more likely to buy Super.

194
00:16:37,920 --> 00:16:51,120
Or it also balances out, of course, like if, for example, it's showing you the same, it doesn't really show you the same Super ad three times, but it's showing you Super ad three times that clearly you're not interested in, it backs out off and shows you other ads.

195
00:16:51,840 --> 00:16:57,680
So it's basically built to optimize LTV with a likelihood to purchase being the objective function.

196
00:16:58,080 --> 00:17:09,120
Many apps that have ads in them don't do this kind of thing, but like as cheap as the CPMs are these days, I think all developers should be experimenting with putting ads in for the premium product.

197
00:17:09,360 --> 00:17:16,640
It's a perfect way to kind of balance that monetization of if you have a freemium product and you're not making that much on ads, which you just don't.

198
00:17:16,960 --> 00:17:24,640
And actually, I'll follow up with a question here, but it's a great opportunity to like remind people, hey, there is a paid product..

199
00:17:25,200 --> 00:17:27,680
There are features you can get and highlighting the benefits.

200
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:32,880
I've seen quite a few of the ads early in the journey when I was first using Duolingo and they're really good.

201
00:17:33,600 --> 00:17:37,440
They really highlight well, like, hey, you're doing great, but you can do even better.

202
00:17:37,680 --> 00:17:39,280
And they really highlight the product features.

203
00:17:39,440 --> 00:17:47,600
And so I think that's a great opportunity for freemium developers who do have ads to be exploring that more to kind of push that upgrade to the subscription.

204
00:17:48,080 --> 00:17:48,800
100%.

205
00:17:49,040 --> 00:17:52,960
And I think the other reason I like our ads is they're just better than programmatic ads.

206
00:17:53,040 --> 00:17:54,880
It's like a better user experience.

207
00:17:55,040 --> 00:17:56,320
It's just a better user experience.

208
00:17:56,560 --> 00:18:04,160
I mean, I think if I had a magic wand, I'd rather have no programmatic adsic ads within my app and only internally produce ads because they look better.

209
00:18:04,400 --> 00:18:11,200
And we're also building kind of lore for our world characters through, even though they might be talking about super, they're also cracking jokes about their own.

210
00:18:11,600 --> 00:18:14,480
For example, we have this kind of controversial super ad.

211
00:18:14,800 --> 00:18:24,720
I don't know if you've seen this one where Junior, one of our characters, is in a bathroom stall and he likes peeks and like makes a roll joke because there's a toilet roll that he's like peeking under.

212
00:18:25,040 --> 00:18:31,280
It's kind of weird, but it's like we're also kind of entertaining a little bit with the ads we produce because we have produced them with that intent.

213
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:34,000
It's much better than showing programmatic ads.

214
00:18:35,040 --> 00:18:39,680
How does admonetization do perfect per user compared to subscription?

215
00:18:40,080 --> 00:18:49,600
And I would assume part of the idea with these partnerships and other things you're working on is to continue pushing that monetization better and better for those free users.

216
00:18:50,240 --> 00:18:57,200
So for us, if you look at Dualingo statistics, subscription revenue is far and above the biggest chunk of our revenue.

217
00:18:57,440 --> 00:19:01,600
So I think it's above, above eighty percent, and ads is a much smaller percentage.

218
00:19:01,760 --> 00:19:07,680
But if you look at payers, about among our DAU base, actually MAU base, it's about ten percent.

219
00:19:07,840 --> 00:19:09,680
So ten percent of our users subscribe.

220
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:12,880
However, subscription is more than eighty percent of our total revenue.

221
00:19:13,760 --> 00:19:37,280
So we focus naturally when it comes to what we think about for monetization, mostly on the subscription side of things because we believe it has one much it's already a much larger business but also much higher potential because we still feel like with any of these actually but certainly still even with the subscription that has now become a sizable business, there's just so much more to do that is making the subscription better.

222
00:19:37,920 --> 00:19:50,080
And also with the freemium app, you realize over time, and this is a lot of experimentation we've done, one is like building the product to be better, so making our subscription features more valuable, but there's a lot of work to be done and also making sure you can you can communicate those well.

223
00:19:50,400 --> 00:19:51,440
We touched on videos.

224
00:19:51,760 --> 00:20:02,160
Videos certainly help because that gives you a format where you can actually say, hey, this other thing that you could buy has all these features because there's not a lot of other easy places to tell that story.

225
00:20:02,720 --> 00:20:09,360
On top of that, there's like how you design your purchase pages makes a difference, for example, how users enter that flow makes a difference.

226
00:20:09,600 --> 00:20:13,040
So anyway, that's in my head how you're communicating your subscription.

227
00:20:13,680 --> 00:20:22,160
So we're putting more and more of our monetization energy into those pieces, meaning make the subscription better and more useful and make sure we can communicate it well.

228
00:20:22,800 --> 00:20:24,320
That tends to have higher returns for us.

229
00:20:24,320 --> 00:20:27,120
I think there's a better return for us in general as a business than ads.

230
00:20:27,360 --> 00:20:29,520
So most of our efforts are on subscriptions.

231
00:20:29,840 --> 00:20:29,920
Yeah.

232
00:20:30,240 --> 00:20:32,720
I mean, you know, Facebook's numbers are just insane.

233
00:20:32,960 --> 00:20:45,760
You know, I think I saw like 250 per user per year in the US, which is about what a Duo Plus subscription costs, but the time in the app, the way they're able to do their bid structures and things like that.

234
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:48,320
But I imagine there's still a lot of room to grow.

235
00:20:49,040 --> 00:20:55,600
But it's interesting that at Duolingo scale, the subscription opportunity is still so much better than the ad monetization.

236
00:20:55,920 --> 00:20:56,320
I think so.

237
00:20:56,480 --> 00:21:04,640
And I think with social media apps, the reality is it's fairly easier, I think, as a product problem to integrate ads into a feed.

238
00:21:05,520 --> 00:21:15,760
And in a product where users are there not for a particular utility, but they are there a lot of times to pass time or they don't even know why they're there.

239
00:21:15,920 --> 00:21:21,120
So I think it's way easier to increase ad load in a way that is not disruptive to how the user feels.

240
00:21:21,840 --> 00:21:28,560
When you are there on an app for a particular utility and for us, a lot of users are there to learn a topic, well, not a lot.

241
00:21:28,800 --> 00:21:30,560
All of our users are there to learn something.

242
00:21:31,520 --> 00:21:38,080
I think increasing ad load is not particularly that tolerated, I guess.

243
00:21:38,400 --> 00:21:42,320
So I think that creates a challenge for a challenge and opportunity, honestly.

244
00:21:42,400 --> 00:21:47,760
For us, subscription just fits as a better business model than increasing ad load the way Facebook might be doing.

245
00:21:48,880 --> 00:21:51,600
One last question on ads, and then we'll move on to the subscription.

246
00:21:52,000 --> 00:22:01,920
But one of your, the product managers and your team came on the podcast, I think, eighteen months ago, and he talked about holdouts that you all do long term holdouts.

247
00:22:02,240 --> 00:22:04,320
Did you all hold out on ads?

248
00:22:04,480 --> 00:22:11,920
Are there people today, is there like 0.001 percent of subscribers that still don't have ads that you can measure against the people who do have ads?

249
00:22:12,160 --> 00:22:17,360
Not anymore, but we did run, I think, almost like a year or two year long holdout for ads.

250
00:22:18,240 --> 00:22:30,320
We do run long holdouts, although I don't recommend year long holdouts because what also eventually happens is you forget that you have a holdout and then eventually you introduce, since you're not testing it every day, you introduce a bug to it.

251
00:22:30,480 --> 00:22:35,680
So like, even though you have a year long holdout, you realize at some point you actually polluted it because you introduced bugs there.

252
00:22:36,000 --> 00:22:50,560
Anyway, so very long holdouts we are anti just because of a practical perspective, but we do way more like three months, six month holdouts on things that we know have different short tool or we assume will have short to long term effects that might be different.

253
00:22:50,960 --> 00:23:06,960
One of those, for example, this is not exactly monetization, but anything that has to do with our leaderboards, we try to run long period experiments or long holdouts because on the Duolingo leaderboard set, if you start from the lowest league, it takes you ten weeks to get to the highest.

254
00:23:07,600 --> 00:23:15,440
If we change the dynamics of how the leaderboard works, we might not see the actual result in two weeks, so we kind of let these run for longer.

255
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:24,640
Another one is push notification experiments where every, of course, new push notification you introduce has amazing returns because it has amazing novelty.

256
00:23:25,200 --> 00:23:33,120
But then if you wait out six weeks, sometimes it doesn't just come back to neutral, it actually turns negative because you're sending too many push notifications that turns people off.

257
00:23:33,360 --> 00:23:38,480
So some stuff that we assume not just monetization might have longer term adverse effects.

258
00:23:38,960 --> 00:23:41,520
We run long holdouts or just run a long experiment.

259
00:23:42,160 --> 00:23:43,840
The way Duolingo tests things is fast.

260
00:23:44,000 --> 00:23:48,960
We could talk hours just on that, but I did want to get back to the monetization journey.

261
00:23:49,760 --> 00:23:53,520
So at what point did it become clear that you needed to introduce a subscription?

262
00:23:53,760 --> 00:23:59,920
And this was pretty early in the, you know, Apple famously opened up subscriptions to all apps in 2016.

263
00:24:00,480 --> 00:24:02,640
I believe you launched the subscription pretty soon after that.

264
00:24:03,120 --> 00:24:03,440
Seventeen.

265
00:24:03,760 --> 00:24:07,680
So what was the story from, okay, ads are starting to work.

266
00:24:08,400 --> 00:24:14,160
We haven't pissed off all the users, but now we're going to actually start charging.

267
00:24:15,120 --> 00:24:34,480
So the the journey we went through was ads was the first experiment, and then after ads was introduced, as a good PM, I was reading every review that was written on, on Duolingo that included the App Store or Reddit or our own forums, and there was a significant amount of people that were saying, can I please pay you guys?

268
00:24:34,640 --> 00:24:36,400
I don't want to see ads, and I love Duolingo.

269
00:24:36,400 --> 00:24:38,080
I get so much value out of it.

270
00:24:38,240 --> 00:24:40,000
It wasn't like a hateful I hate ads.

271
00:24:40,080 --> 00:24:43,440
It was more like I would love to give you guys a few dollars just to remove my ads.

272
00:24:43,760 --> 00:24:46,000
And we thought that was a good idea.

273
00:24:46,400 --> 00:24:49,680
And subscriptions were quite early for mobile apps at the time, you're right.

274
00:24:49,920 --> 00:24:54,800
Majority of the app wisdom was generally you want to sell IEPs.

275
00:24:54,960 --> 00:24:58,720
And I think games obviously had kind of popularized that and that was pretty common.

276
00:24:59,120 --> 00:25:00,800
Subscription apps were earlier.

277
00:25:01,440 --> 00:25:06,720
There was the big like streaming and entertainment, but outside of that, there wasn't a lot of digital subscription behavior.

278
00:25:07,040 --> 00:25:11,360
So we took that idea to heart, but we said, we're not sure if the subscription thing is for us.

279
00:25:11,520 --> 00:25:14,880
We were, our mind was still like., we probably gonna have an IAP model.

280
00:25:15,280 --> 00:25:24,240
So we tested an IAP, which was honestly, like looking back, it's so janky and it's not clear why people bought this, but we said you can buy an ad free month of Duolingo.

281
00:25:24,320 --> 00:25:28,160
So for thirty days we'll turn off the ads, but it's a one time purchase.

282
00:25:28,480 --> 00:25:33,040
And we tested different price points, we tested three dollars, five dollars, and ten dollars.

283
00:25:33,920 --> 00:25:35,680
And everyone thought this kind of wouldn't work.

284
00:25:36,000 --> 00:25:39,200
Like, it's like, why would people pay this one off bizarre exchange?

285
00:25:39,760 --> 00:25:44,880
Honestly, the uptake was kind of good and way more than we expected.

286
00:25:45,040 --> 00:25:48,960
So we were like, clearly there is some demand for turning off ads.

287
00:25:49,680 --> 00:25:53,840
Even though we created the most bizarre package to sell, people want to buy this.

288
00:25:54,560 --> 00:25:55,840
Let's make it a subscription.

289
00:25:56,240 --> 00:26:00,880
But to make it a subscription, just no ads felt slim as a subscription service.

290
00:26:01,680 --> 00:26:03,680
So then we built offline capabilities.

291
00:26:04,080 --> 00:26:08,560
So we said you can use Duolingo fully offline and turn off your ads.

292
00:26:09,040 --> 00:26:13,120
And then when we designed the purchase page, we were like, this looks really weird with two features.

293
00:26:13,280 --> 00:26:17,520
We need a third feature, but we couldn't actually at the time figure out what the third feature should be.

294
00:26:17,920 --> 00:26:27,360
So we kind of wrote a line there that was the thing that users were telling us, which is support Duolingoolingo's mission for short.

295
00:26:28,080 --> 00:26:30,480
And that was our first entry into subscription.

296
00:26:30,560 --> 00:26:31,680
That was Duolingo Plus.

297
00:26:32,000 --> 00:26:42,400
And honestly, what I think we saw happen was probably the majority of users early on bought it for the fact that they enjoyed Duolingo so much and wanted to contribute back.

298
00:26:42,640 --> 00:26:46,960
The other half, or maybe the half of the demand was from no ads and minimally for offline.

299
00:26:47,760 --> 00:26:50,240
And that was, that kind of started us giving us a run rate.

300
00:26:50,400 --> 00:26:55,520
Obviously, I mean, I'm happy to talk about it more, but from then to today, the subscription has evolved a lot.

301
00:26:55,920 --> 00:26:57,360
But that was the very first version.

302
00:26:57,680 --> 00:26:58,640
Yeah, that's fascinating.

303
00:26:58,800 --> 00:27:00,480
And I do want to get to that journey.

304
00:27:02,880 --> 00:27:05,520
I mean, again, this was 2017.

305
00:27:06,000 --> 00:27:09,200
So best practices and everything else hadn't fully evolved.

306
00:27:09,680 --> 00:27:11,280
And this was just an experiment.

307
00:27:12,320 --> 00:27:14,400
But how, how did you start evolving that over time?

308
00:27:14,640 --> 00:27:31,120
What were, what were some of the, and maybe just kind of give us the highlights of the last almost decade now, eight, eight years, the kind of biggest unlocks when we added this to the paywall, when we added this new tier, what were some of those big unlocks over the past eight years that really kind of moved the business forward?

309
00:27:31,840 --> 00:27:34,800
There's a few, and let me try to think ten years is a lot of time.

310
00:27:35,040 --> 00:27:42,880
There's a few levers that have, looking back, have really either caused step function increases immediately or over time it really compounded.

311
00:27:43,200 --> 00:27:51,920
So one of them, I guess, now if you work in subscriptions, now this is like a no brainer, but at the time it really wasn't having free trials is one.

312
00:27:52,480 --> 00:27:55,360
I actually as a PM, I resisted free trials for a long time.

313
00:27:55,520 --> 00:27:57,200
I assumed it would like, completely flop.

314
00:27:57,680 --> 00:27:58,560
The product was already free.

315
00:27:59,040 --> 00:27:59,840
Yes, exactly.

316
00:28:00,000 --> 00:28:05,200
It's like, why would people want to try free trials for the exact same product they've used for years?

317
00:28:05,440 --> 00:28:06,160
The product is free.

318
00:28:06,320 --> 00:28:15,120
Like, if we were a fully premium product, free trial in my head made a lot of sense, but for freemium, like you said., I thought it didn't make sense.

319
00:28:15,280 --> 00:28:16,080
Turns out I was wrong.

320
00:28:16,240 --> 00:28:18,160
Free trials are really helpful.

321
00:28:18,960 --> 00:28:27,440
For the obvious skeptical user that might not want to pay upfront, but they are interested in trying it out, it just makes it so easy to just jump in and try.

322
00:28:27,920 --> 00:28:28,640
It's very intuitive.

323
00:28:28,800 --> 00:28:32,320
Now as a user, I love free trials because I'm like, well, I don't want to commit to pay.

324
00:28:32,480 --> 00:28:33,040
Let me give it a try.

325
00:28:33,440 --> 00:28:38,480
Were there specific things that were then behind the paywall that people needed to experience?

326
00:28:39,920 --> 00:28:40,160
Not really.

327
00:28:40,560 --> 00:28:40,720
Really?

328
00:28:40,880 --> 00:28:44,880
I mean, offline you can say offline, but it's offline is the same as online.

329
00:28:45,040 --> 00:28:46,640
It just works when you're in airplane mode.

330
00:28:46,880 --> 00:28:55,280
So if you imagine this subsccription which is no ads and offline, you don't really need to experience it, but I think it just removes the payment hesitation.

331
00:28:55,520 --> 00:28:57,360
So it lowers the barrier to entry.

332
00:28:57,600 --> 00:28:58,720
So you're not committing payment.

333
00:28:58,800 --> 00:29:00,000
You're like, I'll give it a try.

334
00:29:00,640 --> 00:29:02,880
And I think that's the real benefit of free trials.

335
00:29:03,200 --> 00:29:11,280
So at this point we have, we're very pro free trials and I think our purchase flows are now designed to highlight how our free trial works as a mechanic.

336
00:29:11,520 --> 00:29:16,240
We even have designed pages which communicate, hey, there's a seven day free trial on day five.

337
00:29:16,480 --> 00:29:19,440
We're going to send you a notification to see if you still want your subscription.

338
00:29:19,600 --> 00:29:26,720
Like, because I, and this has performed well in AB tests because I think users have a subscription aversion.

339
00:29:27,040 --> 00:29:34,240
And I think in a lot of countries, maybe not as much the US, there's also subscription scam aversion where you click on something you somehow can't get out.

340
00:29:34,720 --> 00:29:44,080
I think if you highlight how what a free trial is, one is like you don't have to pay anything really, and out of what the mechanics of it, it relieves this subscription aversion.

341
00:29:44,480 --> 00:29:49,360
Well, while we're on the topic, I know you recently did an experiment with the try for free button.

342
00:29:49,920 --> 00:29:53,600
We've been experimenting with a lot on everything our purchase pages.

343
00:29:54,080 --> 00:29:56,400
And maybe it's worth talking about, you mentioned AB testing.

344
00:29:56,560 --> 00:29:59,520
Maybe I'll take us on a tangent for ten seconds.

345
00:30:00,000 --> 00:30:03,280
So Duolingo currently runs somewhere around 400 AB tests.

346
00:30:03,440 --> 00:30:08,480
So the Duolingo app itself is heavily evolving real estate.

347
00:30:08,880 --> 00:30:13,840
And a good chunk of those, not majority, but a good chunk of them also happen on our purchase flow.

348
00:30:14,080 --> 00:30:28,880
So if you look at any element, whether that's like Duo's flight path on the super page or what the copy we write, how it's highlighted, how it's underlined, what the button color is, what the button copy is, all of those elements, we have teams trying to figure out how to make them better.

349
00:30:29,120 --> 00:30:31,600
And of course, for the record, we're talking a lot about monetization.

350
00:30:31,840 --> 00:30:33,840
That's not the only place in the app where we're doing this.

351
00:30:34,000 --> 00:30:35,600
Like our onboarding flow is the same.

352
00:30:35,680 --> 00:30:37,920
Our session end is the same., our lessons are the same.

353
00:30:38,080 --> 00:30:43,600
So we take kind of maximum care to improve all these flows, but monetization is no exception.

354
00:30:44,240 --> 00:30:46,480
But that button copy, we've experimented a lot.

355
00:30:46,800 --> 00:30:50,480
And that is one learning that I honestly didn't have or didn't really expect.

356
00:30:51,280 --> 00:30:56,240
What you say in the purchase button makes a massive difference on your revenue curves.

357
00:30:56,560 --> 00:30:57,840
We have tested many things.

358
00:30:58,080 --> 00:31:03,280
There's a couple of magic strings that were like, you look at the graph and you're like, there must be a measurement error.

359
00:31:03,520 --> 00:31:05,200
Like, why are the numbers so green?

360
00:31:05,840 --> 00:31:09,680
The two specifically were we went from, I think, start my free trial.

361
00:31:10,000 --> 00:31:11,680
So I think we said something like.

362
00:31:14,160 --> 00:31:15,760
That was that was your baseline.

363
00:31:16,000 --> 00:31:19,840
And then we tried many things, but one of the big uplifts was start my free trial.

364
00:31:20,320 --> 00:31:20,880
That was very good.

365
00:31:21,200 --> 00:31:22,960
And then we tried, what did we say?

366
00:31:23,440 --> 00:31:24,160
Try for free.

367
00:31:24,400 --> 00:31:24,800
That was better.

368
00:31:25,440 --> 00:31:27,520
And then we tried try for zero.

369
00:31:28,080 --> 00:31:28,480
zero zero.

370
00:31:30,080 --> 00:31:35,680
Weirdly, that is the string that I think hacks your brain to really grock that that button is entirely free.

371
00:31:36,320 --> 00:31:36,480
Zero.

372
00:31:36,960 --> 00:31:38,480
zero like two zero decimals.

373
00:31:38,720 --> 00:31:41,280
Now I think that's what's live in the app if I'm not mistaken.

374
00:31:41,600 --> 00:31:42,880
And that performed really well.

375
00:31:43,200 --> 00:31:47,680
And just changing try for free to try for dollar sign zero.

376
00:31:48,320 --> 00:31:51,760
zero zero zero.00 You saw a meaningful uplifting conversion.

377
00:31:52,160 --> 00:31:52,800
Correct, yes.

378
00:31:53,600 --> 00:31:58,720
And it was, I guess, the start trial conversion, but then also the trial conversion to pay.

379
00:31:59,120 --> 00:32:00,640
So like both numbers went up.

380
00:32:00,960 --> 00:32:01,280
Correct.

381
00:32:01,440 --> 00:32:01,520
Wow.

382
00:32:01,920 --> 00:32:03,120
Yeah, it's so crazy.

383
00:32:03,360 --> 00:32:04,480
Yeah, it was unexpected.

384
00:32:04,480 --> 00:32:07,280
Honestly, it's like you wouldn't think this makes that much of the difference.

385
00:32:07,760 --> 00:32:19,120
I think one other thing I've learned with freemium, and honestly, with any subscription and paywall behavior is a lot of when you go and design these flows, you're designing with logic, right?

386
00:32:19,360 --> 00:32:24,160
You're saying, let me communicate my features so whoever is going to buy should read them and understand what they're buying.

387
00:32:24,720 --> 00:32:29,520
So they can do the logical exchange of if I part with this many dollars, I will get this many features.

388
00:32:30,000 --> 00:32:32,240
That's how you go and design a flow.

389
00:32:32,640 --> 00:32:36,560
But the reality is humans don't experience mobile flows that way at all.

390
00:32:37,360 --> 00:32:44,400
It's way more based on emotion and reaction than, oh, let me read and think about the value exchange of what is happening here.

391
00:32:44,640 --> 00:32:45,840
It's way more emotional.

392
00:32:46,400 --> 00:32:51,040
So I think these things that are kind of sound like, you know, they're saying the same thing, right?

393
00:32:51,280 --> 00:32:55,840
Zero is that zero and free are the same thing, but they create different emotions and make a difference.

394
00:32:56,240 --> 00:32:56,880
That's fascinating.

395
00:32:57,360 --> 00:33:11,840
Well, we've dug down multiple levels of rabbit hole, but to surface this back up in thinking through the past ten years, were there any other kind of key unlocks, specific features that you put behind the paywall that really moved the needle?

396
00:33:12,480 --> 00:33:12,880
Definitely.

397
00:33:13,120 --> 00:33:14,720
So we talked about free trial.

398
00:33:15,600 --> 00:33:25,200
Another thing for us that was quite a meaningful turn in our evolution was honestly putting more useful features behind our subscription.

399
00:33:25,360 --> 00:33:27,920
So I started with saying, okay, we had ads and offline.

400
00:33:28,720 --> 00:33:33,680
We started adding more, we had two features that were basically transformational to the subscription.

401
00:33:34,560 --> 00:33:43,520
One of them was Duolingo had this pacing mechanic called Hearts, now it's called Energy, but Hearts was not integrated with our subscription at all.

402
00:33:43,760 --> 00:34:04,320
The way it works is you can go through Duolingo and do as much as you want, but if you make a certain number of mistakes, you start running out of hearts, and then before you can do new content, you have to regenerate hearts, and you can do that by doing practice lessons, or you can do that by watching ads, or at the time we said you can renew your hearts with an in-app purchase.

403
00:34:04,960 --> 00:34:11,040
We decided to integrate the Hearts mechanic with our subscription and said, hey, if you subscribe, you actually get unlimited hearts.

404
00:34:11,360 --> 00:34:16,400
That was a big turning point because it turns out a lot of users don't want to practice old content.

405
00:34:16,560 --> 00:34:17,680
They just want to do new content.

406
00:34:18,160 --> 00:34:25,680
Even though practice is good for your learning, we said, okay, if you would like, this is you can choose your optionality if you subscribe.

407
00:34:26,080 --> 00:34:34,160
That was a good turning point because it made the feature set of Duolingo Plus at the time much more integrated and much more useful.

408
00:34:34,800 --> 00:34:38,000
Another feature we created was again around practice, custom practice.

409
00:34:38,160 --> 00:34:41,040
So we created a feature that is a separate tab called Practice Hub.

410
00:34:41,840 --> 00:34:43,680
We made that part of Super at the time.

411
00:34:43,840 --> 00:34:46,160
Actually, it's now, became fully free.

412
00:34:46,400 --> 00:34:50,800
We now gave it all to free users because we realized it should be free, so we did that.

413
00:34:50,960 --> 00:34:58,880
But at the time, we said, if you'd like to do very custom practice on the things you've learned on Duolingo, here's an extra place where that's served to you as an inventory.

414
00:34:59,120 --> 00:35:03,680
So you can go back and practice anything you'd like in whatever order you like rather than the order we prescribe.

415
00:35:04,320 --> 00:35:17,920
Anyway, this is a very long-winded way to say feature improvements that expanded the feature set of Duolingo Plus was naturally a win on two fronts, both conversion and retention of the subscription, which is great.

416
00:35:18,160 --> 00:35:18,560
We love that.

417
00:35:18,880 --> 00:35:20,160
And that gave us a lot of scale.

418
00:35:21,040 --> 00:35:26,080
Other than that, honestly, it is rare to have put like one silver bullet that improved things.

419
00:35:26,640 --> 00:35:30,400
For us, a lot of this has been continuous AB testing improvement.

420
00:35:30,720 --> 00:35:38,800
I think we talked about the copy version as one thing, but imagine that happening across every bit and piece of our purchase flow.

421
00:35:39,200 --> 00:35:46,080
And I think that's why Dualingo's purchase flows get copied a lot because I think people have heard us say this, I say this a lot, which is like we did AB test many things.

422
00:35:46,560 --> 00:35:51,360
So if you want to copy something, probably you're copying something that has been tested at least for Dualingo.

423
00:35:51,520 --> 00:35:56,000
It probably won't work for most apps, but you can look at us because we've tested stuff.

424
00:35:56,240 --> 00:35:56,960
That's fascinating.

425
00:35:57,360 --> 00:36:00,640
Before we move on, I did want to talk one more angle of monetization.

426
00:36:01,120 --> 00:36:08,400
I know by the time this podcast is aired, you'll have taken something that was previously paid and moved it into the free tier.

427
00:36:08,560 --> 00:36:08,720
Why?

428
00:36:08,960 --> 00:36:09,360
Why do that?

429
00:36:09,840 --> 00:36:15,680
So let me talk about actually two features that we one of them we just did and we are about to do.

430
00:36:15,920 --> 00:36:34,160
So Practice Hub I talked about, we have realized that number one as a goal, we would like to provide more value to our free users because free users, there's the missionary reason why we want to teach more people, but there's the business reason which is free users are our marketing engine.

431
00:36:34,560 --> 00:36:39,040
Duolingo grows as a whole user base and as a business through word of mouth.

432
00:36:39,280 --> 00:36:45,200
Word of mouth is much stronger if your free product is stronger because you might or may not pay irrelevant.

433
00:36:45,360 --> 00:36:48,800
If you're using Duolingo, you tell your friends about Duolingo and say, This is awesome.

434
00:36:48,880 --> 00:36:49,840
I'm learning Spanish.

435
00:36:50,400 --> 00:36:50,880
They try it.

436
00:36:51,040 --> 00:36:51,440
They do the same.

437
00:36:51,520 --> 00:36:52,960
So that's how Duolingo really grows.

438
00:36:52,960 --> 00:36:59,520
That's the majority of our growth engine and that growth engine, of course, gets stronger if we can provide more value to free users.

439
00:36:59,840 --> 00:37:05,760
So it's a delicate balance if you're a freemium product to decide how much value to put on each side, right?

440
00:37:06,240 --> 00:37:10,720
And we certainly want to put value as much as possible to our free user base.

441
00:37:11,040 --> 00:37:14,240
So that's the overarching reason for why.

442
00:37:15,200 --> 00:37:19,120
But the two features that we did was Practice Sub and the second one is Explain My Answer.

443
00:37:19,600 --> 00:37:25,840
These are really, I mean, I love these features because they are really additive to your Dualingo experience.

444
00:37:25,920 --> 00:37:40,160
So Practice Sub, we talked about, I'm not going to repeat it, that is now actually already live with free users and Explain My Answer is moving from being a Dualingo Max feature, which is our highest tier, to free users because we have realized the revenue value is not that high.

445
00:37:40,640 --> 00:37:43,040
So we want, meaning it doesn't make that much revenue.

446
00:37:43,600 --> 00:37:44,480
How did you determine that?

447
00:37:44,720 --> 00:37:45,280
We tested it.

448
00:37:46,640 --> 00:37:53,600
We tested giving it for free and compared it to a B condition or a control condition where it is part of Max.

449
00:37:54,320 --> 00:38:04,240
The impact, well, there was impact, but we decided to kind of, you know, reduce our revenue but rather provide value for our free users because we believe that's the right thing to do in the long term.

450
00:38:04,640 --> 00:38:23,360
And there's one more reason which is when we first introduced this feature, it was financially impossible for us to make it free because it uses LLM calls to explain basically, well, what it does for the record is you give an answer, you answered something, and we basically use LLMs to describe to you what you got wrong for your specific answer.

451
00:38:23,520 --> 00:38:26,720
So it's very why there is an LM that is used.

452
00:38:27,120 --> 00:38:28,240
LM costs were really high.

453
00:38:28,480 --> 00:38:35,760
So we had to put it into a subscription just to provide the service, but costs have gone down significantly and we've improved how we cache things.

454
00:38:36,960 --> 00:38:44,720
So since costs came down, our only decision was revenue loss and revenue loss we decided to take to provide this service for.

455
00:38:45,200 --> 00:38:45,280
Yeah.

456
00:38:45,840 --> 00:38:59,040
It seems like that's a real theme is being very strategic about what's free and what's paid because of that balance because you want to create that amazing free experience, but you still monetize.

457
00:38:59,360 --> 00:39:02,240
And so that tension must exist be difficult as a product team.

458
00:39:02,720 --> 00:39:08,240
Yes, and you know, honestly, the hardest part is for me, it's really easy.

459
00:39:08,400 --> 00:39:10,240
I think for our like CEO, it's very easy.

460
00:39:10,400 --> 00:39:12,160
We always want to do the right thing for the user.

461
00:39:12,800 --> 00:39:17,120
It's not hard for us to think about, okay, like, how do we improve the free experience?

462
00:39:17,280 --> 00:39:18,160
There's some obvious things.

463
00:39:18,800 --> 00:39:23,200
The hardest part is taking a loss in an experiment is not intuitive.

464
00:39:23,440 --> 00:39:31,360
So the teams that are working on these are finding it hard to be like, I understand all this good stuff you said, but we're going to drop our metric.

465
00:39:31,680 --> 00:39:33,280
It's like, it doesn't feel intuitive.

466
00:39:33,840 --> 00:39:36,560
So I think convincing the organ, convincing the teams and explaining.

467
00:39:37,120 --> 00:39:46,960
why this is the right thing to do long term is the hard part because you can imagine the positive side of what I'm saying doesn't really have a metric, but the cost of it has a very real metric.

468
00:39:47,360 --> 00:40:00,240
So I think that they run hand in hand, but I think you have to realize, especially if you're a freemium business, is if you only go by positive revenue metrics, you will burn yourself to the ground.

469
00:40:01,920 --> 00:40:16,640
I don't want to call out specific companies, but you can see a lot of subscription businesses that have effectively nickel and dime their users if they're freemium and then burned their growth engine, even though their revenue kept growing, but that only works for like some amount of years, but eventually your growth stops.

470
00:40:17,280 --> 00:40:21,440
I think for us what's most important is our growth engine, our user growth engine keeps growing.

471
00:40:22,000 --> 00:40:25,360
That's why you have to make these hard calls sometimes to be like, we're going to take a loss.

472
00:40:25,520 --> 00:40:30,640
It's okay because we're helping our growth engine is the convincing part is the hard part.

473
00:40:31,120 --> 00:40:35,760
How do those calls get made when an AB test shows a benefit?

474
00:40:36,400 --> 00:40:47,840
And I imagine this happens in more than just these monetization features, but how do you balance the kind of art of product management against the science of product management when you run 400 simultaneous AB tests?

475
00:40:48,160 --> 00:40:48,720
This is a great question.

476
00:40:49,200 --> 00:40:55,440
I think this happens a lot for us, I would say, because we have this other side of our equation as a business.

477
00:40:55,680 --> 00:41:02,720
So we talked about user growth and monetization, that's the same for every app, but Dualingrad has one more unique element, which is learning and learning efficacy.

478
00:41:02,880 --> 00:41:09,520
So we run AB tests to try to improve our learning efficacy, and the problem with that is it doesn't have a single metric you can put on a graph.

479
00:41:10,080 --> 00:41:20,160
Even if you could, I mean, we use proxy metrics, there's no perfect metric for it, but even if you have proxy metrics that are going up, let's say your learning goes up, but your revenue comes down, should you exchange that?

480
00:41:20,400 --> 00:41:21,360
Should you not exchange that?

481
00:41:21,680 --> 00:41:29,680
There's these where you move away from a mostly purely science to, as you said, the art of product management.

482
00:41:30,000 --> 00:41:31,120
How do these decisions get made?

483
00:41:31,440 --> 00:41:43,360
If they are really consequential, it's like, okay, the revenue loss might be large, or there's a DAU hit, whatever it is, like the clear business hit, we have to bring in most senior people to decide if as a company we should take this tradeoff.

484
00:41:43,680 --> 00:41:48,240
Like that means our CEO would be certainly involved in saying, okay, committed, let's do this.

485
00:41:48,720 --> 00:41:53,040
If it's at smaller scale, I think most of our teams understand these tradeoffs fairly well.

486
00:41:55,200 --> 00:42:00,160
If it's an exchange of, for example, clearly the thing we're doing helps you improve your conversation skills.

487
00:42:01,040 --> 00:42:03,840
But in turn, maybe it makes our content a little harder.

488
00:42:04,400 --> 00:42:07,200
So maybe it hurts some of our business metrics.

489
00:42:07,360 --> 00:42:10,400
But if the amount is small, teams can independently make those calls.

490
00:42:10,880 --> 00:42:18,640
If the magnitude is large, but it's a strategic decision we should make, then I think we bring in senior people to sign off is kind of how we operate.

491
00:42:19,200 --> 00:42:21,760
And this is why Duolingo is such a well-loved app.

492
00:42:22,240 --> 00:42:32,960
I mean, hearing you explain that and understanding how many product decisions have been made over the years to provide that better experience at the expense of DAU or monetization or other things.

493
00:42:33,280 --> 00:42:34,160
That's why people love it.

494
00:42:34,240 --> 00:42:53,360
And that's like you said, you it's hard to put a metric onric on that love and the social sharing and the feeling a user gets when they open the app, but it sounds like that's something you protect very carefully as a company, which then pays dividends over the much longer term, even if you can't always AB test your way to that.

495
00:42:54,240 --> 00:42:54,960
That's very well said.

496
00:42:55,360 --> 00:43:12,240
We have this operating principle that we I love and we repeat a lot is take the long view, meaning you can make business decisions very differently if your goal is to be a very profitable or a very fast growing company for two years versus if you want to build a company for that will be around 100 years.

497
00:43:12,800 --> 00:43:14,800
I think those are very different decisions.

498
00:43:14,880 --> 00:43:20,480
And to be specific, you could monetize the Duolingo feature set very heavily if you wanted to.

499
00:43:20,640 --> 00:43:22,240
And for two years, you'll make a lot of money.

500
00:43:22,720 --> 00:43:23,840
But one, that's not our goal.

501
00:43:23,920 --> 00:43:25,680
And two, that would be terrible for free users.

502
00:43:26,160 --> 00:43:29,280
But three, we will burn ourselves to the ground if we do that.

503
00:43:29,520 --> 00:43:32,800
Like we cannot build a hundred year business by taking more and more.

504
00:43:33,440 --> 00:43:40,640
That's what a lot of freemium apps fall into this premium trap where like the easiest win you can always do is like, oh yeah, let me take some free features, make it paid.

505
00:43:40,880 --> 00:43:42,560
And immediately all your metrics are green.

506
00:43:42,720 --> 00:43:45,360
And that does kind of work for six months or maybe a year.

507
00:43:45,840 --> 00:43:47,040
But eventually it stops working.

508
00:43:47,200 --> 00:43:52,080
The other thing it does is it creates opportunity for someone else to do it for free.

509
00:43:52,480 --> 00:43:57,840
The Duolingo story, the very early one that we didn't really talk about is why Duolingo grew so much early on.

510
00:43:59,040 --> 00:44:08,400
At the time, there were tools teaching languages, there were the Rosetta Stones of the world, but they were paid and expensive, so being free and fun created a massive differentiator.

511
00:44:09,120 --> 00:44:17,840
If we were to tomorrow wake up and say, all right, we're going to charge for all of our great features, very likely in two years, another great app will do it for free.

512
00:44:18,160 --> 00:44:27,840
So I think preserving the free value is just a mote that we have to keep and we want to keep for Mich missionary reasons, but for business reasons also makes a lot of sense.

513
00:44:28,080 --> 00:44:29,920
Yeah, that's such a great answer.

514
00:44:30,720 --> 00:44:37,600
Well, I did want to move on to the new products that Duolingo has been introducing and kind of becoming a multi product company.

515
00:44:38,560 --> 00:44:42,080
For many apps, it's a big leap.

516
00:44:42,320 --> 00:44:51,840
The most famous example is Calm finding sleep stories and that secondary product market fit being this new lever of growth that really moved the needle for them.

517
00:44:52,480 --> 00:45:00,240
So tell me about why you decided to start adding new courses and we can talk through like how that went and what you added and everything.

518
00:45:01,680 --> 00:45:03,200
Honestly, it's been a really fun journey.

519
00:45:03,840 --> 00:45:07,760
It's been now, has it been four years since we introduced our new subject?

520
00:45:08,000 --> 00:45:08,960
Maybe three to four.

521
00:45:10,160 --> 00:45:13,280
But now we have math, music, and chess on top of languages.

522
00:45:14,320 --> 00:45:23,280
The reason we chose those specific subjects, they're all varied and they have their own thesis, but at its core they have a few properties that carry over.

523
00:45:24,080 --> 00:45:27,360
One is they could be taught on mobile in a fun way.

524
00:45:27,840 --> 00:45:29,120
Not every topic qualifies.

525
00:45:29,520 --> 00:45:35,040
For example, coding, we considered, but it's really hard to teach on mobile, so we decided not to invest there.

526
00:45:35,040 --> 00:45:38,240
That doesn't mean we won't ever do it., but today it felt too hard to do.

527
00:45:38,880 --> 00:45:43,200
Another one is they all have large markets of people that are already learning these subjects.

528
00:45:43,360 --> 00:45:46,560
So we're not trying to create the market necessarily, but we are.

529
00:45:47,040 --> 00:45:48,160
There's already existing demand.

530
00:45:49,040 --> 00:45:49,440
That's two.

531
00:45:50,080 --> 00:45:52,080
And three, and I think this is connected to mobile.

532
00:45:52,480 --> 00:46:00,800
Our expertise in language, which is at its core, if you think about it, a bite sized fun experience, can carry over fairly well to these subjects.

533
00:46:01,440 --> 00:46:11,680
The anti example of this would be stuff that is done offline, like running could have been something we decided to do, but that is an offline experience that you tie with an online experience.

534
00:46:11,680 --> 00:46:14,560
That is not exactly how we're used to doing bite size exercises.

535
00:46:14,720 --> 00:46:18,320
So we wanted to make sure our competencies played well in these subjects.

536
00:46:19,040 --> 00:46:21,200
So these topics would fit really well.

537
00:46:21,200 --> 00:46:24,000
We actually looked around quite a bit before we invested in each of them.

538
00:46:24,560 --> 00:46:35,200
The maybe the fourth internal reason these topics is each of them had one or more people inside the company that were quite tenured, like tenured builders that really wanted to build these.

539
00:46:35,680 --> 00:46:37,840
So they became a kind of a seed for these projects.

540
00:46:38,320 --> 00:46:43,520
So I did want to get into the staging of this because you first launched Math as a separate app, right?

541
00:46:43,920 --> 00:46:46,320
So what was the thesis behind doing that?

542
00:46:46,640 --> 00:46:50,240
And then what didn't work it work out that you now brought it back into the app?

543
00:46:51,040 --> 00:46:51,120
Yes.

544
00:46:51,680 --> 00:46:54,160
Our thesis changed actually over time.

545
00:46:54,400 --> 00:46:59,040
So when we launched Math, we assumed our future would be separate apps.

546
00:46:59,200 --> 00:47:09,520
Like our strategy, we because at the time we also actually had, we still do, but we had another app called Duolingo ABC that is meant to teach literacy to younger children.

547
00:47:09,920 --> 00:47:13,520
So teaching their first language rather than the second language that Duolingo teaches.

548
00:47:13,920 --> 00:47:17,760
But our strategy was headed in a direction where all these new topics would be separate apps.

549
00:47:18,480 --> 00:47:29,840
We thought at the time, this is partly true still, but our thinking certainly evolved that different audiences would seek different apps rather than be in one mega app for multiple topic learning.

550
00:47:30,000 --> 00:47:39,840
So if you want to learn math, we thought the overlap between you and the language learner would be slim, and that's why you would probably want a separate app for doing one job.

551
00:47:40,320 --> 00:47:44,880
And I don't know if this was common wisdom at the time, but it felt really good as a strategy.

552
00:47:46,080 --> 00:47:50,480
We did realize one thing as time went on, which is actually two realizations happened.

553
00:47:50,640 --> 00:47:54,960
One turns out a lot of people want to learn multiple things at once.

554
00:47:55,520 --> 00:47:58,560
At least Duolingo learners want to learn multiple things at once.

555
00:47:58,720 --> 00:48:00,320
So it became an inconvenience actually.

556
00:48:00,320 --> 00:48:02,000
It's actually to switch between apps.

557
00:48:02,240 --> 00:48:06,320
And for two apps, maybe you could survive, but if it's three, four apps, you're just not going to do it.

558
00:48:06,720 --> 00:48:14,240
And there's just this inherent human demand that we realize exists within Duolingo, which is not just like, I'm here to learn Spanish.

559
00:48:14,480 --> 00:48:19,200
There's certainly a lot of users that are there for one thing, but also a lot of users are there for self improvement in general.

560
00:48:19,520 --> 00:48:29,920
So it was a natural behavior that we just saw happening, which is, I'll learn a little bit of math, I'll learn a little bit of chess, I'm there to spend my good screen time on Duolingo to improve myself.

561
00:48:30,320 --> 00:48:35,920
It is less relevant exactly what that topic is, that might change over time, as long as I'm improving myself in something.

562
00:48:36,000 --> 00:48:41,840
That kind of led us towards, well, let's make it easy for these folks to do that.

563
00:48:42,560 --> 00:48:43,520
So that was one.

564
00:48:44,160 --> 00:48:56,240
The other is just the business reality of growing a separate app is extremely expensive while we were like, why are we playing hard mode when we can play easy mode because in the same app you have a user base.

565
00:48:56,800 --> 00:49:03,360
It's much easier to get a user that is in the main app to try something new than to get them to download an entirely new app.

566
00:49:03,920 --> 00:49:05,280
It's so crazy to hear that from you.

567
00:49:05,360 --> 00:49:12,160
I mean, of all the companies with, you know, tens of millions of users and beloved and everything else that the separate app was hard mode.

568
00:49:12,160 --> 00:49:13,840
It's hard mode, for sure.

569
00:49:14,080 --> 00:49:16,240
And I think a lot of companies have come to this conclusion.

570
00:49:16,560 --> 00:49:20,400
I think there are successful and unsuccessful examples of both of these, actually.

571
00:49:20,640 --> 00:49:27,920
But the famous examples are, I think, Uber and Facebook have done both separate and combined apps in multiple forms.

572
00:49:28,240 --> 00:49:39,760
And that's why I don't think there's the right answer for every company, but for us, this, if you think about user behavior, which is generally, there's a lot of users that are around self improvement and let me learn something and that will change over time.

573
00:49:40,560 --> 00:49:43,200
It just made sense for them to be in one app.

574
00:49:44,000 --> 00:49:45,520
How did you solve the product problem?

575
00:49:45,920 --> 00:49:48,720
I think one of the challenges of the product.

576
00:49:48,720 --> 00:50:00,720
of mobile is that you have such tiny real estate to communicate everything to shoehorn a feature into this tiny space that's already crowded by other things.

577
00:50:01,120 --> 00:50:02,880
How did you solve the product problem of it?

578
00:50:04,640 --> 00:50:07,360
A lot of with a lot of work is the short answer.

579
00:50:08,080 --> 00:50:17,520
And before we launched anything, these projects all took probably from six, like somewhere from six to twelve months to go from the first idea to full launch.

580
00:50:17,680 --> 00:50:24,560
So that means in the meantime, we probably designed, tested, prototyped, redesigned, prototyped, tested again on ourselves and some.

581
00:50:24,560 --> 00:50:32,080
And we've seen it ourselves and some beta users maybe many, many times until we got to the state where it's like, okay, within the confines of this mobile experience, this feels fun enough.

582
00:50:32,240 --> 00:50:44,960
It's actually teaching something useful and it's good enough to go in the app and be on par with the quality of where the language courses are because language courses have been improving for thirteen years, whereas this new thing is starting from scratch.

583
00:50:45,200 --> 00:50:48,800
So anyway, a lot of dog fooding and prototyping, I think is answer number one.

584
00:50:49,360 --> 00:50:54,800
But the other one is, I think you can eventually teach a lot of things on mobile.

585
00:50:55,360 --> 00:50:57,840
There are some courses that are easy, there are some courses that are harder.

586
00:50:58,000 --> 00:50:59,600
Music is really hard, I would say.

587
00:51:00,240 --> 00:51:04,400
Chess is fairly easier because you have one visual element that is the chessboard.

588
00:51:04,800 --> 00:51:10,080
As long as you fit that into the mobile experience, you can build a lot of stuff on it, but really, it's now contained.

589
00:51:10,480 --> 00:51:17,360
Music is much harder because we're, for example, teaching you piano skills on a screen, and we have to think a lot more creatively on how to do that.

590
00:51:17,760 --> 00:51:28,320
And you can imagine the amount of stuff we've tried and still are trying to improve, which is you can't fit an entire keyboard on a piano, for example, and you also can't fit it when the phone is vertical, you have to turn it horizontal.

591
00:51:28,800 --> 00:51:35,520
To fit more key space, we actually tilted the keyboard a little a few degrees so it gives you a little bit more angle for touch space.

592
00:51:36,160 --> 00:51:39,520
So all that stuff is like innovation that you end up having to figure out.

593
00:51:39,920 --> 00:51:41,520
And I would say your music is the hardest.

594
00:51:41,680 --> 00:51:48,800
Chess is chess and math are easier because math follows a very similar visual style to in exercises language.

595
00:51:49,200 --> 00:52:02,640
How do you surface it to users in the app in a way that doesn't get in the way of the people who just want to learn a language or are just there for music or crowding the screen real estate with these multiple things?

596
00:52:03,040 --> 00:52:04,000
How do you solve that problem?

597
00:52:04,720 --> 00:52:12,560
We've made it fairly integrated so it doesn't feel like you're crossing into a different app, but it feels like you're just switching.

598
00:52:13,040 --> 00:52:13,760
what you're learning.

599
00:52:14,000 --> 00:52:16,960
So we had already built a system basically to switch courses.

600
00:52:17,040 --> 00:52:20,960
So if you were learning Spanish, you could switch to learning French or Italian, any other language.

601
00:52:21,600 --> 00:52:32,880
In that system, we now also offer with some different hierarchy, of course, on like you could switch to French, which is the second language you learn the most, or there's your other topics now, which are math, music and chess.

602
00:52:33,200 --> 00:52:40,880
We integrated there and honestly, it's worked fairly well because we do already have a lot of users that learn multiple languages per day.

603
00:52:41,600 --> 00:52:42,800
And they started discovering it.

604
00:52:43,040 --> 00:52:46,400
We also did some light promos just to raise awareness on the topics.

605
00:52:46,880 --> 00:52:53,920
That said, this remains a bit of an uns of an unsolved problem, if I'm being honest, which is a lot of our users have no idea that we also teach math, music and chess.

606
00:52:54,320 --> 00:53:03,440
So next year, we're still going to try to figure out how to make sure that at least our user base that's coming to the app every day is aware that these topics exist.

607
00:53:03,680 --> 00:53:05,120
Yeah, it's a tough, tough problem.

608
00:53:05,680 --> 00:53:08,480
How do you think about competition with Duolingo?

609
00:53:09,280 --> 00:53:16,080
Do you think of YouTube and Netflix and other screen time as competition or who competes with Duolingo?

610
00:53:17,200 --> 00:53:21,040
I think any screen time is probably our realistic competition.

611
00:53:24,560 --> 00:53:32,640
You would probably think, well, education, any education app, but realistically, one, we are already way bigger than any education app.

612
00:53:32,880 --> 00:53:34,560
So at least user base-wise.

613
00:53:34,640 --> 00:53:40,480
So we don't particularly see behavior of, oh, I stopped using Duolingo and now I'm using an education app.

614
00:53:40,960 --> 00:53:49,840
Most of any churn or any user that we lose goes to spend time on Instagram or YouTube or anything that is fun in their phone.

615
00:53:50,080 --> 00:53:54,160
So for us, I think real competition is good screen time or I guess good or bad.

616
00:53:54,320 --> 00:54:03,520
I think that's subjective, but anything that is spent in screen time is Duolingo's compet why we spend so much time on making Duolingo fun.

617
00:54:04,400 --> 00:54:09,760
It is also, I would say, it's headwinds to try to take something that is brain intensive.

618
00:54:09,920 --> 00:54:12,400
Like learning a language is not an easy activity.

619
00:54:12,560 --> 00:54:14,320
It's not as passive as scrolling on your feed.

620
00:54:14,480 --> 00:54:22,880
It's actually like burning your brain a little bit if you do it for a long time, and we try to make it as easy as fun because otherwise, well, you'll go to Instagram.

621
00:54:23,040 --> 00:54:23,840
You'll go to TikTok.

622
00:54:24,000 --> 00:54:26,080
There's many engaging things that are just pulling you.

623
00:54:26,720 --> 00:54:44,080
So that's how, if I'm being honest, we don't really think about competition because it's so weird to think about screen time, but if you were to define it, it's really sc honestly the case for most apps these days, unless it's a specific utility where you need to like get in, get out, solve a very specific problem.

624
00:54:44,400 --> 00:54:56,240
If it's any kind of even habit forming app, I mean, almost any app, when you launch your home screen, are you pulled to Instagram or are you pulled to Duolingo to do your lessons and it's tough.

625
00:54:56,800 --> 00:55:00,640
How do you think of AI as competition and will AI just assume everything?

626
00:55:00,880 --> 00:55:04,880
Nobody learns a language because they've got their AirPods automatically translating.

627
00:55:05,680 --> 00:55:07,760
I imagine that's a hot topic right now.

628
00:55:08,320 --> 00:55:11,680
Well, I get this question a lot and I actually really love it because I think.

629
00:55:12,960 --> 00:55:17,200
We have a pretty clear thesis with two parts of this question.

630
00:55:17,440 --> 00:55:21,200
So one part is, will AI remove the need for learning languages?

631
00:55:21,680 --> 00:55:26,080
And in that case, will Duolingo, well, what will Duolingo do if that happens?

632
00:55:26,480 --> 00:55:31,920
The other one is, I think, will AI disrupt Duolingo because AI has language teaching capabilities?

633
00:55:32,320 --> 00:55:33,280
I'll start with the first one.

634
00:55:33,840 --> 00:55:43,360
I think the reality is being able to teach a language is not particularly a new part of a new ability for a tool to have.

635
00:55:43,600 --> 00:55:46,640
What I mean by that is textbooks also can teach you a language.

636
00:55:46,880 --> 00:55:51,040
Now AI can, which is for a technology perspec perspective, it's incredibly impressive it can do that.

637
00:55:51,520 --> 00:55:57,600
But text, it is not enough, unfortunately, to remove the need to learn languages.

638
00:55:57,840 --> 00:56:09,200
People we've learned, and I'm only talking about languages, we can talk about other subjects, that the reason people learn languages is incredibly varied, so you might be learning to connect with your heritage.

639
00:56:09,840 --> 00:56:13,200
AI doesn't really solve that at the end.

640
00:56:13,360 --> 00:56:14,640
You still will want to learn languages.

641
00:56:14,880 --> 00:56:16,560
This is similar to when calculators came out.

642
00:56:16,720 --> 00:56:18,000
We didn't stop teaching math.

643
00:56:18,320 --> 00:56:19,120
It's quite similar.

644
00:56:19,760 --> 00:56:24,640
And the need to learn languages does not disappear even if you have massively good simultaneous.

645
00:56:25,520 --> 00:56:27,280
translation and AI can do that really well.

646
00:56:27,600 --> 00:56:33,280
So that's, I think, we don't see language learning demand going anywhere and we certainly don't see it post LLMs either.

647
00:56:33,840 --> 00:56:40,000
The other question is, there are now other tools that are non-duolingo, like the chat GPTs of the world that can actually teach you language.

648
00:56:40,160 --> 00:56:49,760
If you ask it to teach me Spanish, it will do something and I think that's very fair, but I think that my textbook analogy still applies there where it can teach you a language, but is it exciting?

649
00:56:50,800 --> 00:56:51,680
Is it fun enough?

650
00:56:52,160 --> 00:56:57,920
And until that is the case, a thing being able to teach you a language is not a threat to our business.

651
00:56:58,560 --> 00:57:03,280
I think we don't see that it that much and we continue investing in making things fun for that reason.

652
00:57:03,760 --> 00:57:13,040
And maybe the third thing I will add is we see AI only as an accelerator to Duolingo because we use AI to do mainly two things that make Duolingo better.

653
00:57:13,360 --> 00:57:17,360
One, produce content at scale, at higher quality and higher volume.

654
00:57:17,920 --> 00:57:27,280
The other is introduce features that we couldn't do before that solve some of the teaching problems that we really care about solving, like conversation practice.

655
00:57:28,240 --> 00:57:36,320
So in a way, AI has helped us a lot and I think it will continue to, and we will continue building better products with AI.

656
00:57:36,800 --> 00:57:39,200
So we're happy in everything that's happening.

657
00:57:39,440 --> 00:57:42,240
Well, let's move on to a few of your contrary takes.

658
00:57:42,720 --> 00:57:46,960
So don't build for a persona, build for the general population.

659
00:57:47,520 --> 00:57:47,680
Why?

660
00:57:48,080 --> 00:57:49,920
I mean, it's the classic PM thing.

661
00:57:50,160 --> 00:57:54,000
Build a persona, figure out who you're talking to, all that kind of stuff.

662
00:57:54,480 --> 00:57:59,440
Yeah, this is any PM framework would say, don't build for a general audience.

663
00:57:59,680 --> 00:58:01,040
Always choose who you're building for.

664
00:58:02,000 --> 00:58:09,040
I think when building consumer products, at least this is certainly Duolingo, I don't want to generalize too much, but we have we we are like very anti persona.

665
00:58:09,360 --> 00:58:49,440
So if someone comes in from the outside world, joins our team most of the time they their instinct is like oh yeah like i'm gonna build some persona maps i'm gonna call this person the wanderer the other one the explorer and which one am i gonna build for and we're immediate like please don't do that like stop because i think if you want it really depends on what kind of product you want to build if you're trying to get to a billion users which we we have kind of the the ambition to you have to eventually build for a general population that's that's like i think my theory number one theory number two is with language learning if you pick a persona you're actually really cutting off the rest of your audience and language learning is such a long tail of reasons for learning.

666
00:58:50,000 --> 00:58:55,920
Someone could be learning to get a job, someone could be learning to move countries, someone could be learning to connect with their heritage.

667
00:58:56,320 --> 00:58:57,360
These are extremely varied.

668
00:58:57,600 --> 00:59:03,280
So if you pick one and say, Oh, this is really important, yeah, like the common PM framework would say, That's how you succeed.

669
00:59:03,360 --> 00:59:12,240
I think for us, that's how you fail because then you've ignored basically the whole long tail of why people are learning languages and you've built for a very specific one.

670
00:59:12,800 --> 00:59:21,040
So if the use case of a product is varied, which a lot of consumer products have that property actually, you have to figure out how to build for the general population.

671
00:59:21,600 --> 00:59:22,000
That's great.

672
00:59:23,040 --> 00:59:24,400
Next one, people don't read.

673
00:59:25,440 --> 00:59:26,880
Okay, this is another favorite one.

674
00:59:27,600 --> 00:59:29,600
This kind of connects to something I hinted at.

675
00:59:30,400 --> 00:59:44,720
Most early career or honestly, even people who join Dualingo with experience, product designers or PMs fall into this trap of what they write on the screen as copy and text, think is read by the user.

676
00:59:46,080 --> 00:59:50,000
The reality is on mobile apps, people's attention span is already shrunk.

677
00:59:50,240 --> 00:59:53,920
It's like you might be cooking with one hand and doing your app with the other hand.

678
00:59:54,320 --> 00:59:55,280
You might be on the toilet.

679
00:59:55,360 --> 00:59:58,560
There's all kinds of situations where now your attention span is like shrunk.

680
01:00:00,480 --> 01:00:06,960
You're kind of going through an experience by not kind of paying attention to every detail, but the core, maybe like the thirty percent of a screen.

681
01:00:07,520 --> 01:00:08,640
So we always repeat this.

682
01:00:08,880 --> 01:00:10,000
Of course it's an exaggeration.

683
01:00:10,320 --> 01:00:16,480
Of course people read some things on mobile, but really in a graphical it interface, they read very little.

684
01:00:17,280 --> 01:00:21,840
But to simplify my like extreme hot take is people don't read, so don't rely on text.

685
01:00:22,400 --> 01:00:24,320
Rely on great design to guide the user.

686
01:00:24,800 --> 01:00:30,400
And maybe the better said version of this is if you need to write a tutorial on what to do, you've already lost it.

687
01:00:30,560 --> 01:00:35,280
So it needs to be design that leads the user, not the text and the labels that you wrote.

688
01:00:36,000 --> 01:00:39,040
Okay, well, let's close out with the three questions I ask every guest.

689
01:00:39,840 --> 01:00:44,720
What was the most impactful experiment, change or something you implemented this year, your biggest win?

690
01:00:45,520 --> 01:00:51,920
It's not exactly this year, it's been two years, but this is worth calling is the introduction of video call on Duolingo.

691
01:00:52,400 --> 01:01:05,680
I've been using Duolingo to improve my Spanish for nine years now, and this is the one feature I can confidently say it's helped me speak Spanish much more than before, and I mean we're very excited for what it can do.

692
01:01:06,320 --> 01:01:11,680
It still feels very early, but already if you use it, you can just speak Spanish better, and I feel great about that.

693
01:01:12,160 --> 01:01:24,400
That's such a great example of building a truly great product experience with AI., a lot of people are slapping it on and trying to put a chatbot in their app where it doesn't make sense.

694
01:01:24,560 --> 01:01:31,440
And the video call, which is really such a great example of taking this new technology and actually creating a great customer experience.

695
01:01:31,520 --> 01:01:33,360
So it's cool that you brought that up as such a win.

696
01:01:34,000 --> 01:01:35,120
What about the biggest fail?

697
01:01:36,080 --> 01:01:52,960
The biggest fail is this is not, I don't think this went very public, but actually what a lot of our community doesn't remember or don't know is before video call, our first attempt at like a big speaking feature was a human-based tutoring product.

698
01:01:53,600 --> 01:01:57,440
So it was a higher tier subscription because it was quite expensive for us to logistically provide.

699
01:01:57,920 --> 01:02:01,120
But you would press a button, I think we called it, did we call it tutors?

700
01:02:01,520 --> 01:02:06,000
But it was our higher tier subscription, but you would press a button, you would connect with a Spanish tutor on demand.

701
01:02:06,320 --> 01:02:07,360
No booking required.

702
01:02:08,000 --> 01:02:09,360
Honestly, it was pretty awesome.

703
01:02:09,680 --> 01:02:12,080
Like for speaking practice, it was really awesome.

704
01:02:12,560 --> 01:02:18,320
It had this one massive problem, which is no one wanted to do it.

705
01:02:19,680 --> 01:02:21,280
It was really scary.

706
01:02:22,240 --> 01:02:28,720
At Duolingo, imagine this super fun experience and we would tell you, hey, you are going to connect with a human, you are going to practice Spanish.

707
01:02:28,880 --> 01:02:30,560
People would press it, they would dial in.

708
01:02:30,800 --> 01:02:34,480
We would see their faces come online and they would be like, oh shit, get me out of here.

709
01:02:34,880 --> 01:02:39,200
Because one, it's really scary to speak in Sak in Spanish, like on the language you're learning.

710
01:02:39,440 --> 01:02:43,600
And two, there's another real human on the other side that was extremely jarring.

711
01:02:43,760 --> 01:02:49,840
We iterated a lot to make you into it, but eventually we gave up because humans just didn't want to do that.

712
01:02:50,080 --> 01:02:52,960
And actually, this brings me to another hot take that I do want to mention.

713
01:02:53,920 --> 01:02:56,640
Do not take user feedback for face value.

714
01:02:57,440 --> 01:03:00,320
For years, this is what people asked us to build.

715
01:03:00,480 --> 01:03:03,920
Like, we're like, please build me a system where I can talk to a human tutor.

716
01:03:04,240 --> 01:03:05,520
We built it, no one used it.

717
01:03:05,840 --> 01:03:10,320
It's such a great example of a great feature, but just it doesn't work.

718
01:03:10,560 --> 01:03:19,360
And like, as product people, we can still get into our heads, take feedback and build something that's truly great but it doesn't work.

719
01:03:19,440 --> 01:03:20,880
So that's such a great one.

720
01:03:21,200 --> 01:03:22,080
All right, last question.

721
01:03:22,800 --> 01:03:28,960
Growth would be easier if I think growth would be easier if two things were true.

722
01:03:30,000 --> 01:03:45,360
One is pixel perfect design didn't matter in consumer because I think consumer app design bar has become so high that if you want to make progress and lead product-led growth, you have to get pixel perfect design that will go out to users.

723
01:03:45,600 --> 01:03:51,520
It can't be just a wireframe looking button that you put in your app or your illustration is not really cared for.

724
01:03:53,120 --> 01:03:54,640
This increases the time to ship products.

725
01:03:54,960 --> 01:03:59,120
So that's one pixel perfect design does matter and it growth would be way easier.

726
01:03:59,680 --> 01:04:04,800
Maybe this was the case when apps first came out, but it matters and it makes a difference.

727
01:04:05,360 --> 01:04:14,480
And I think two, if AI really contributed to productivity in both prototyping and coding.

728
01:04:14,720 --> 01:04:20,800
And what I mean by that is I think we are seeing today in the ranges of 10 to 20 percent productivity improvements.

729
01:04:21,040 --> 01:04:21,840
That's really impressive.

730
01:04:22,240 --> 01:04:28,080
But like when LLMs first came out, my hope was that they would be kind of 10x improvers.

731
01:04:28,320 --> 01:04:31,760
And I think if you've got the Silicon Valley hype today, that's what the messaging is.

732
01:04:31,920 --> 01:04:34,960
But really, I'm looking at what we're doing and what every company is doing.

733
01:04:35,360 --> 01:04:39,680
Really, the speed up of the product pipeline is in the ten percent to twenty percent range.

734
01:04:40,160 --> 01:04:46,400
Growth would be easier if we got what was promised and it was two hundred percent improvement, but I don't think that's the case.

735
01:04:46,480 --> 01:04:51,200
Okay, well as we wrap up, anything else you wanted to share, any jobs you wanted to shout out or anything?

736
01:04:52,000 --> 01:04:53,040
I will definitely do that.

737
01:04:53,600 --> 01:05:00,800
We are currently hiring for honestly, we're always hiring, but currently we're hiring for quite a bit of product manager positions.

738
01:05:01,280 --> 01:05:08,080
I think the audience of this show probably is interested in monetization since we also talked about it, and we're also hiring for monetization product managers.

739
01:05:08,400 --> 01:05:10,000
They're all on our careers website.

740
01:05:10,240 --> 01:05:10,560
Nice.

741
01:05:10,720 --> 01:05:13,200
Yeah, I think that would be a great job for this for this audience.

742
01:05:13,440 --> 01:05:19,200
So if you're really into subclub, you're probably really into monetization, so apply.

743
01:05:19,360 --> 01:05:21,360
Okay, Jamie, it was so much fun talking to you today.

744
01:05:21,520 --> 01:05:22,560
Thank you so much for joining me.

745
01:05:22,720 --> 01:05:23,920
Thank you so much for having me.

746
01:05:24,080 --> 01:05:24,720
This is awesome.

747
01:05:27,120 --> 01:05:28,480
Thank you so much for listening.

